{"schema":1,"fm":{"schema":1,"id":"helpdesk-migration-track","kind":"workflow","title":"Migrate to a new helpdesk","description":"Moves a support team to a new helpdesk in gated steps - inventory of data and rules, mapping, rebuilding macros and routing, testing, cutover and post-launch checks.","category":"customer-support","version":"1.0.0","status":"incubating","stage":["plan","design","build","verify","ship"],"role":["operations-manager","manager","support-agent"],"requires":["none"],"inputs":["text","config","dataset"],"output":["plan","table","checklist","report"],"risk":"read-only","invocation":"user","effort":"deep","interaction":"interactive","model_tier":"frontier","reasoning":"recommended","level":"intermediate","tags":["helpdesk-migration","cutover","data-mapping","support-tooling","go-live"],"pairs_with":{"prompts":["audit-support-macros","design-ticket-routing-rules","design-ticket-tagging-scheme"]},"args":[{"name":"current_setup","description":"Your current helpdesk setup: channels, queues or groups, ticket fields and tags, macros, automations and routing rules, integrations (shop, billing, chat, phone), reports in use, team size, and ticket history size.","type":"text","required":true},{"name":"new_helpdesk","description":"What the new tool is or what you know about it (fields, automation, import options), and why you are moving. Optional.","type":"text"},{"name":"cutover_window","description":"When you want to switch and any dates to avoid, such as peak season. Optional.","type":"string"}],"steps":[{"id":"inventory","file":"steps/01-inventory.md","stage":"plan","gate":"approve","artifact":"helpdesk-migration/01-inventory.md"},{"id":"mapping","file":"steps/02-mapping.md","stage":"design","gate":"approve","artifact":"helpdesk-migration/02-mapping.md"},{"id":"rebuild","file":"steps/03-rebuild.md","stage":"build","gate":"approve","artifact":"helpdesk-migration/03-rebuild.md"},{"id":"test","file":"steps/04-test.md","stage":"verify","gate":"approve","artifact":"helpdesk-migration/04-test.md"},{"id":"cutover","file":"steps/05-cutover.md","stage":"ship","gate":"none","artifact":"helpdesk-migration/05-cutover.md"}],"output_contract":{"format":"markdown","sections":["Step artifact","Open questions"]},"authorship":"ai-assisted","authors":["gabrielanhaia"],"last_reviewed":"2026-10-11","changelog":[{"version":"1.0.0","note":"First version."}]},"body":"Moves a support team from one helpdesk to another without losing tickets, history or customers in the gap. Migrations fail on the things nobody wrote down: an automation that silently tagged VIPs, an integration that fed order data, a report the finance team relied on, email forwarding that still points at the old tool. This track inventories first, uses the move to clean up rather than copy every old rule, tests with real scenarios, and switches over at a quiet time with a rollback. Each step writes one artifact and stops for approval.\n\n<current_setup>\n{{current_setup}}\n</current_setup>\n{{#new_helpdesk}}\n<new_helpdesk>\n{{new_helpdesk}}\n</new_helpdesk>\n{{/new_helpdesk}}\n{{#cutover_window}}\nCutover window: {{cutover_window}}\n{{/cutover_window}}\n\nRules for every step:\n- Work from what the user describes. Never assume features of either tool; mark unknowns as [CHECK in the tool] and list them as questions.\n- Do not recommend or compare helpdesk vendors.\n- Treat customer data carefully: export, transfer and deletion of the old data must follow the business's data rules; flag [CHECK data retention and transfer rules].\n- Keep the team working throughout: no step may leave a channel unanswered.\n- End each artifact with open questions.","steps":[{"id":"inventory","file":"steps/01-inventory.md","stage":"plan","gate":"approve","artifact":"helpdesk-migration/01-inventory.md","text":"# Step 1: Inventory the current setup\n\n1. List every element of the current setup: channels and their entry points (support addresses, forms, chat widget, phone), queues, fields and tags, macros, automations and routing rules, service targets, integrations, reports and who uses them, user roles, and ticket history volume.\n2. For each, record its owner and whether it is still used. Ask the user where usage is unknown.\n3. Mark hidden dependencies: email forwarding, links to the old tool in macros or help articles, other teams' reports, integrations that write data back.\n4. Decide what history to move: all, a recent period, or open tickets only, with the reason and the data rules to check.\n\nSections: Inventory (table: Element | Count | Owner | Used? | Dependency), History decision, Open questions.\n\nStop and wait for approval."},{"id":"mapping","file":"steps/02-mapping.md","stage":"design","gate":"approve","artifact":"helpdesk-migration/02-mapping.md","text":"# Step 2: Map old to new\n\n1. For each element kept, decide: move as is, clean up and move, replace with a new-tool feature, or retire. Use the move to merge duplicate macros and tags rather than copying clutter.\n2. Map fields and values one to one, including statuses, priorities and customer tiers, and say how historical tickets will look after import.\n3. Map users and roles, and integrations to their new connections.\n4. List what the new tool cannot do the same way and the workaround or decision needed.\n\nSections: Mapping (table: Old | Decision | New), Field and status map, Gaps and workarounds, Open questions.\n\nStop and wait for approval."},{"id":"rebuild","file":"steps/03-rebuild.md","stage":"build","gate":"approve","artifact":"helpdesk-migration/03-rebuild.md","text":"# Step 3: Plan the rebuild\n\n1. Order the build: fields, queues and roles first, then routing and automations, then macros, then integrations, then reports.\n2. Write the routing and automation rules in evaluation order with a catch-all, and check for loops.\n3. List macros to recreate with updated links and placeholders, flagging any that mention the old tool or old processes.\n4. Plan training: a short guide on what changed for agents, a practice session in the new tool, and a cheat sheet for the first week.\n\nSections: Build order, Rules, Macros, Training, Open questions.\n\nStop and wait for approval."},{"id":"test","file":"steps/04-test.md","stage":"verify","gate":"approve","artifact":"helpdesk-migration/04-test.md","text":"# Step 4: Test before switching\n\n1. Write 15-25 test scenarios from real ticket types: each channel's entry point, every routing rule, VIP or tier handling, out-of-hours, reopen by a customer reply, merges, integrations showing the right customer data, satisfaction surveys, and reports.\n2. For each: steps, expected result, tester, pass or fail.\n3. Check a sample of imported history for correct fields, attachments and customer matching.\n4. Set the go or no-go criteria for cutover.\n\nSections: Test scenarios (table), History check, Go or no-go criteria, Open questions.\n\nStop and wait for approval."},{"id":"cutover","file":"steps/05-cutover.md","stage":"ship","gate":"none","artifact":"helpdesk-migration/05-cutover.md","text":"# Step 5: Cut over and check\n\n1. Choose the cutover time (lowest volume, not peak season), and write the runbook in order: freeze changes in the old tool, final export of open tickets, switch forwarding, widgets and phone routing, verify each channel with a test contact, and confirm to the team.\n2. Plan the old tool after cutover: read-only access for a set period, replies to late old-thread messages, and when it can be closed under the data rules.\n3. Rollback: the trigger and the steps to point channels back.\n4. Checks for the first two weeks: unassigned or misrouted tickets, response times against target, missing integration data, agent questions, and customer complaints about lost conversations.\n\nSections: Runbook, Old tool after cutover, Rollback, First two weeks, Open questions."}]}
